# {{PROJECT_NAME}} research workflow

Local workflow revision: {{WORKFLOW_REVISION}}

Adopted from `organize-research-project`: {{SKILL_REVISION}}

This file is the project's adopted workflow. Its policies and source boundaries
apply alongside current user instructions. Changes to the originating skill
require deliberate local adoption; record deviations and affected open work.

## Locations and authority

Paths are relative to the project root unless explicitly absolute or remote.

| Role | Location |
|---|---|
| Maintained theory entry point | {{THEORY_ENTRY}} |
| Shared definitions or notation contract | {{NOTATION_CONTRACT}} |
| Reusable code and reproduction entry point | {{CODE_ENTRY}} |
| Studies and current records | {{STUDIES_ROOT}} |
| Exploratory runs | {{RUNS_ROOT}} |
| Accepted-result reproductions | {{ACCEPTED_RUNS_ROOT}} |

Declare an empty or unavailable entry point explicitly. Treat superseded
material as historical evidence, with its status and replacement identified;
recency alone does not establish scientific authority.

## Study identity and source boundaries

Use the study explicitly assigned by the user. Otherwise continue the current
study only while its scientific question and scope remain the same. A materially
different question normally receives a new study. Several tasks may share a
study with explicit ownership; one task may coordinate several studies with
separate scientific contexts.

A study's default inputs are its own source and evidence, the maintained theory
and code, their designated reproduction inputs, and authorized external sources.
Other studies' unpromoted findings are not established dependencies. Cross-study
use requires explicit authorization and a record of source version, provisional
status, and any loss of independence. Do not bypass this boundary through
summaries, Git history, generated products, or another contributor.

For an independent attempt, use only its agreed problem and common inputs. For
an independent review, supply the frozen candidate and full dependencies without
author discussion or earlier verdicts. Fresh folders or agents alone do not
establish independence. Disclose exposure that compromises the required scope.

## Keep one current record

Use each study's existing README or equivalent. Record the question, scope,
permitted sources, owners, results and evidence links, checks, unresolved gaps,
run recipes, and next authorized action. Preserve useful existing records.

Track scientific status, checking status, and adoption status separately.
Calling a result internally checked requires its actual argument or method,
identified inputs, executed relevant checks, outcomes, and remaining limits.
Empirical checking includes execution from recorded inputs, not only inspection
of an old plot. Keep useful negative and partial results even if never promoted.

## Experiments and generated products

Storage, retrieval, and retention policy:

{{STORAGE_POLICY}}

Before an authorized experiment, record its question, controls, metrics, validity
criteria, seeds or replication rule, budget, and stopping condition. Scale this
record to the work; ordinary regression tests do not require a new campaign.

Give each evidential execution a fresh run identifier and namespace within the
chosen store. Preserve input identity and retrievability, code revision plus
relevant dirty changes, configuration, command and working directory, relevant
environment and numerical settings, exit status, checks, and output locations.
Version essential source, proof, and hand-written configuration outside transient
output storage. A hash identifies bytes but cannot replace retaining inputs.

Trace each reported figure or table to its runs and analysis source. Moving
artifacts requires verifying the destination and updating references. Redrawing
a stored plot is not a fresh reproduction of its upstream experiment. Preserve
failed and interrupted runs with their actual status.

## Promotion into maintained theory and code

Required review roles, depth, acceptance authority, and authorization:

{{ACCEPTANCE_POLICY}}

For each candidate, assess relevance, duplication, and coherent placement.
Prepare its precise claims, assumptions, full arguments, reusable source,
verification, and empirical reproduction recipes. Freeze the candidate and its
dependencies with actual content identities and a neutral review assignment.

Complete the policy's independent reviews and integration checks. Retain input
identities, scope, findings, executed checks, and unresolved objections. Missing
inputs or required reviews remain gaps. Build and reproduce the assembled
addition using maintained inputs, without study-only scripts or private history.

Present the concrete checked package to the designated authority when approval
is required and not already given. After authorization, recheck dependencies and
concurrent work, integrate that package, verify correspondence, and record the
accepted revision and destinations in the originating study. Review alone does
not grant integration permission; prior explicit authorization need not be asked
for again. Partial acceptance must identify which claims remain blocked.

Scientific changes reopen the affected checks under this policy. Reuse a prior
review only for the same inputs and scope. Formatting-only maintenance receives
proportionate checks and cannot introduce unreviewed scientific changes.

## Ownership, concurrency, and handoff

{{CONCURRENCY_POLICY}}

At checkpoints, update the study's current record and next authorized action.
Do not reopen a closed campaign from an old plan. When a correction supersedes a
claim, retain the old evidence with its status, identify the replacement, and
recheck affected downstream dependencies. Record workflow changes and their
effective scope without rewriting historical evidence or approval.
